  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1034 PM MDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows a classic monsoonal pattern with a Four \nCorners high wrapping moisture up out of Arizona into Utah and the \nGreat Basin before turning east across Utah and into Colorado. This \npattern doesn't tend to produce heavy rains across eastern Utah and \nWestern Colorado because the Wasatch Range taps the moisture of the \nlower levels leaving the mid to high level moisture to push east, \nbut this pattern can produce significant lightning with only light \nrain. Otherwise the pattern is controlled by high pressure that the \nmodels are positioning across the southeast, but satellite imagery \nputs it more over southern Louisiana/southeast Texas which is more \nfavorable for pushing the moisture off the Gulf up into West Texas, \nNew Mexico and ultimately southern Colorado. Look for pwats ranging \nfrom 3/4 inch to an inch across the region tomorrow with the heavier \nmoisture to the west  mostly over eastern Utah. Though we are nor \nexpecting heavy rain from these showers and thunderstorms, be aware \nthat the major fire scars can produce significant flash flooding and \ndebris flows with only a half inch of rain falling in under a half \nhour. Always be alert to the weather and your surroundings that may \npose possible hazards. Flash flooding can occur miles from the \nthunderstorm.\n\nTomorrow will see low pressure forming off Southern California that \nwill develop into a trough along the West Coast by Wednesday. This \nwill bring a more south-southwesterly flow up through Arizona into \neastern Utah and Western Colorado with a more direct path for deeper \nmoisture to move up into the region Wednesday night and Thursday \nwith pwats peaking at an inch or more across much of the region. \nThis is when we are likely to see areas of heavy rain and training \nthunderstorms, especially along the southern to southwestern \nexposures of rising terrain. SPC has the entire region under \nmarginal (>5%) chances for excessive rainfall with slight (>15) along \nthe Divide extending west across the higher terrain of central and \nsouthern Colorado, and to the south over southeastern Arizona into \nwest central New Mexico. Would not be surprised if these two areas \nof higher probability for excessive rain connect in the next day or \ntwo. This will be the greatest threat for flash flooding from heavy \nrains and debris flows from the fire scars. Stay alert to changes \nand updates to the forecast over the next few days.\n\nBeyond a few days, forecast confidence fall off as the models, and \neven the ensembles are diverging in their solutions. They are in \nagreement with the flow aloft becoming more southwesterly Friday \nwhich tends to dry out the region, but the European models push the \nhigh back to to the east keeping the Southwest under a drier \nsouthwesterly flow while the GFS/GEFS models keep the high farther \nwest over Texas with a more southerly flow across Arizona and New \nMexico to bring a return of the deeper moisture going into early \nnext week.",
